From: John Chia <john@johnchia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nor: pin the rootfs partition per board, instead of deriving it

`sf probe` read the squashfs superblock at CONFIG_ENV_ROOTADDR and set
rootmtd from bytes_used -- 5120k under 5 MB, 8192k at or above it -- and
set rootsize the same way from ${filesize}. rootmtd is what fills
${rootmtd} in bootargs, so the partition table the kernel was handed, and
with it the offset rootfs_data begins at, was a function of how well one
particular filesystem happened to compress.

Crossing the threshold in either direction moves the start of rootfs_data
by 3 MB. The overlay that had been living at the old offset is then found
unmountable, reformatted on the next boot, and every setting on the camera
is gone -- with nothing on the camera saying why.

It is triggered by ordinary work. Dropping one unused package from a
board's defconfig in the firmware tree moved ssc377qe and ssc377d from
5.9 MB to 4.5 MB, which flips both. ssc30kq builds at 5,189,632 bytes
today, 48 KB under the line: a package that gains 48 KB repartitions that
board and nothing in the change would look related.

Where it is actively unsafe is a board whose part is too small for the
larger table. 2368k precedes the rootfs, so 8192k needs 10560k before
rootfs_data starts, which the 8 MB parts infinity6b0 boards carry do not
have. A 5120k-capped board is size-checked at 5,242,880 and repartitions
at 5,238,784, leaving a 4 KB window where an image passes its build-time
check and still asks for a table the flash cannot hold.

So make it a constant: ROOTMTD and ROOTSIZE per board in sstar-common.h,
next to the flash layout, matching the rootfs cap the firmware tree checks
images against -- infinity6c and infinity6e are 16 MB parts with an 8192k
rootfs, infinity6 and infinity6b0 are 8 MB with 5120k.

WHY THE ASSIGNMENT STAYS IN cmd_sf.c

The obvious version of this deletes the assignment along with the
derivation and puts the size straight into CONFIG_BOOTARGS. That bricks
every camera that has already booted once, which was established the
direct way on an ssc377qe.

A camera that has run saves its environment, and what it saves is bootargs
holding ${rootmtd} unexpanded, plus a rootmtd of its own -- stale, because
the derivation only ever setenv'd in RAM and never wrote it back. The
saved environment wins over the compiled-in default, so removing the
assignment leaves the stale value expanding: a board running an 8192k
table because the derivation said so every boot falls back to the 5120k
its environment has held since the day it was flashed. Its 5.6 MB rootfs
no longer fits the partition, the squashfs mount fails, and panic=20 puts
it in a reboot loop that only serial gets it out of.

So the constant is assigned where the derivation was, on every probe,
which bootcmd runs before it expands bootargs. Nothing is written to
flash. The derivation's own behaviour is the proof this reaches a saved
environment: that camera booted an 8192k table for months while its saved
rootmtd said 5120k.

Carried here rather than in the pinned branch so that the pin stays
commit-identical to openipc/u-boot-sigmastar master.

Signed-off-by: John Chia <john@johnchia.com>
diff --git a/common/cmd_sf.c b/common/cmd_sf.c
index c4f812e..a68739d 100644
--- a/common/cmd_sf.c
+++ b/common/cmd_sf.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_MS_NOR_ONEBIN)
 #include <jffs2/load_kernel.h>
+#include <linux/stringify.h>
 
 extern struct spi_flash* spi_flash_probe_driver(u32 u32_address, unsigned int bus, unsigned int cs,
         unsigned int max_hz, unsigned int spi_mode);
@@ -171,31 +172,25 @@ static int do_spi_flash_probe(int argc, char * const argv[])
 	flash = new;
 #endif
 
-	char buf[64];
-	unsigned int magic, bytes;
-
-	if (spi_flash_read(flash, CONFIG_ENV_ROOTADDR, sizeof(buf), buf)) {
-		printf("Failed to read from SPI flash\n");
-		return 1;
-	}
-
-	memcpy(&magic, &buf[0], sizeof(magic));
-	memcpy(&bytes, &buf[40], sizeof(bytes));
-
-	if (magic == 0x73717368) {
-		if (bytes + 0x1000 < 0x500000) {
-			setenv("rootmtd", "5120k");
-		} else {
-			setenv("rootmtd", "8192k");
-		}
-	}
-
-	ulong file = getenv_ulong("filesize", 16, 0);
-	if (file < 0x500000) {
-		setenv("rootsize", "0x500000");
-	} else {
-		setenv("rootsize", "0x800000");
-	}
+	/* rootmtd sizes the rootfs partition in bootargs, and rootsize is the
+	 * length urwrite erases. Both used to be worked out right here: rootmtd
+	 * from bytes_used in the squashfs superblock at CONFIG_ENV_ROOTADDR, and
+	 * rootsize from ${filesize}. That made the partition table a function of
+	 * how well one filesystem happened to compress -- crossing 5 MB moved the
+	 * start of rootfs_data by 3 MB, and the overlay living at the old offset
+	 * was reformatted on the next boot with nothing saying why.
+	 *
+	 * They are per-board constants now (sstar-common.h), but they are still
+	 * assigned here rather than left to the default environment, and that is
+	 * the load-bearing part: a camera that has booted once has a SAVED
+	 * bootargs holding ${rootmtd} unexpanded, and a saved rootmtd beside it
+	 * that the default environment cannot reach. Assigning it on every probe
+	 * -- which bootcmd runs before it expands bootargs -- is what reaches
+	 * those cameras. Nothing is written to flash; this is the running
+	 * environment only.
+	 */
+	setenv("rootmtd", __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_ROOTMTD));
+	setenv("rootsize", __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_ROOTSIZE));
 
 	return 0;
 }
diff --git a/include/configs/sstar-common.h b/include/configs/sstar-common.h
index 79dac2d..48a793e 100755
--- a/include/configs/sstar-common.h
+++ b/include/configs/sstar-common.h
@@ -32,7 +32,26 @@
 #define CONFIG_ENV_KERNADDR 0x50000
 #define CONFIG_ENV_KERNSIZE 0x200000
 #define CONFIG_ENV_ROOTADDR 0x250000
+
+/* The rootfs partition is a property of the board: it is the size the board's
+ * images are built and size-checked against, so it is spelled out per board
+ * here rather than worked out at boot from whatever happens to be in flash.
+ * cmd_sf.c assigns both on every `sf probe`, which is what reaches a camera
+ * whose saved environment still holds a rootmtd of its own.
+ *
+ * ROOTMTD must equal the rootfs cap the firmware tree checks each image
+ * against, or an image that passes its build-time size check still overruns
+ * the partition it is written to. 2368k precedes the rootfs, so ROOTMTD is
+ * also bounded by the part: 8192k needs a 16 MB flash, and asking for it on an
+ * 8 MB one puts rootfs_data past the end of the chip.
+ */
+#if defined(CONFIG_ARCH_INFINITY6C) || defined(CONFIG_ARCH_INFINITY6E)
+#define CONFIG_ENV_ROOTMTD  8192k	/* 16 MB parts */
+#define CONFIG_ENV_ROOTSIZE 0x800000
+#else					/* infinity6, infinity6b0: 8 MB parts */
+#define CONFIG_ENV_ROOTMTD  5120k
 #define CONFIG_ENV_ROOTSIZE 0x500000
+#endif
 
 #define CONFIG_BOOTARGS "console=ttyS0,115200 panic=20 root=/dev/mtdblock3 init=/init mtdparts=NOR_FLASH:256k(boot),64k(env),2048k(kernel),\\${rootmtd}(rootfs),-(rootfs_data) LX_MEM=\\${memlx} mma_heap=mma_heap_name0,miu=0,sz=\\${memsz} cma=2M"
 #define CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND "setenv bootcmd ${cmdnor}; sf probe 0; saveenv; run bootcmd"
@@ -43,7 +62,7 @@
 	"kernsize=" __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_KERNSIZE) "\0" \
 	"rootaddr=" __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_ROOTADDR) "\0" \
 	"rootsize=" __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_ROOTSIZE) "\0" \
-	"rootmtd=5120k\0" \
+	"rootmtd=" __stringify(CONFIG_ENV_ROOTMTD) "\0" \
 	"cmdnor=sf probe 0; setenv setargs setenv bootargs ${bootargs}; run setargs; sf read ${baseaddr} ${kernaddr} ${kernsize}; bootm ${baseaddr}\0" \
 	"ubnor=${updatetool} ${baseaddr} u-boot-${soc}-nor.bin && run ubwrite\0" \
 	"uknor=${updatetool} ${baseaddr} uImage.${soc} && run ukwrite\0" \
